  "custom_entry_hosts_about": "Hosts no custom entry is ever written or read for, since Rust never writes such a name: an IP address or a name whose last label reads as a number, upper case, a trailing dot, a single label, a label with a leading hyphen, and punycode that does not decode. For each, the value would be the valid script name, the separator, then that host. Public-suffix-only names are formed hosts: the entry form does not judge public suffixes, which the claim refuses (podster_core::hostnames::mappable), so both sides read them the same.",
  "custom_entry_refused_hosts": [
    "192.0.2.1",
    "app.123",
    "app.0x7f",
    "App.Example.com",
    "APP.EXAMPLE.COM",
    "app.example.com.",
    "com",
    "-app.example.com",
    "app-.example.com",
    "xn--zz.example"
  ],
  "custom_entry_formed_hosts": [
    "co.uk",
    "app.example.com"
  ],
  "code_mark_about": "M6 stage 1 (ADR 0010's revision, section 4). A serve entry whose script's release carries code begins with the code mark, then exactly the form a static entry of that host has: the script's name alone on a default host, or the script's name, the separator and the host on a custom one. A static entry carries no mark and is exactly as before. The mark ends in a colon, which no script's name and no host Podster writes can hold, so no static entry of either form can begin with it; it is taken off once, from the start only, and what is left is read as a static entry of that host is. Anything else that looks like a mark (another case, another word, a mark twice, a mark at the end or inside) is not an entry. The private API writes the mark only when told a release carries code, which nothing tells it yet.",
  "code_mark": "code:",
